    <div class="prose">
      The three four-win composers represent three very different archetypes of dominance. <strong>John Barry</strong> spread his wins across 24 years (1966–1990), maintaining relevance across wildly different film eras. <strong>John Williams</strong> packed his wins into an 18-year concentrated stretch (1975–1993) that coincided with the blockbuster age he helped define. <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> has achieved his four wins in just eight years (2018–2026), a concentration of dominance unmatched in Oscar scoring history — and the data ends with him still active.
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      Franz Waxman's back-to-back wins in <strong>1950 and 1951</strong> remain the only consecutive-year double in the category until Alan Menken matched the feat in <strong>1991–1992</strong>. Gustavo Santaolalla then accomplished it again in <strong>2005–2006</strong>, and Göransson in <strong>2023–2024</strong> — the most recent back-to-back in the record.
