<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 4: ERA ANALYSIS ═══════════════ -->
<section id="era">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-num">04</span>
      <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      Four broadly accepted eras provide a useful frame for understanding how the category has changed — not just in who wins, but in what kinds of composers and musical vocabularies the Academy has favored.
    </div>

    <div class="era-grid">
      <div class="era-card golden">
        <div class="era-stripe"></div>
        <div class="era-name">Golden Age</div>
        <div class="era-years">1934 – 1960 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 27 Ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Unique composers</span><strong>26</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Repeat-win ceremonies</span><strong>8 of 27 (30%)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Most wins</span><strong>Tiomkin &amp; Steiner (3 each)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-dominant">
          <strong>Character:</strong> European émigrés dominated — Steiner (Austrian), Korngold (Austrian), Waxman (German), Rozsa (Hungarian), Tiomkin (Ukrainian-born). Lush, late-Romantic orchestral writing was the unquestioned default, drawn directly from the 19th-century symphonic tradition these composers had absorbed in European conservatories.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card silver">
        <div class="era-stripe"></div>
        <div class="era-name">Silver Age</div>
        <div class="era-years">1961 – 1980 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 20 Ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Unique composers</span><strong>18</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Repeat-win ceremonies</span><strong>7 of 20 (35%)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Most wins</span><strong>Barry, Williams, Jarre (2 each)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-dominant">
          <strong>Character:</strong> The era opened with the melodic sophistication of Mancini and Jarre and closed with the disco-inflected synthesizer of Giorgio Moroder (<em>Midnight Express</em>, 1978). John Williams' <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977) signaled the shift to neo-Romantic blockbuster scoring that would define the next era. The diversity of sonic approaches was wider than any previous period.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card modern">
        <div class="era-stripe"></div>
        <div class="era-name">Modern Era</div>
        <div class="era-years">1981 – 2000 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 20 Ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Unique composers</span><strong>18</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Repeat-win ceremonies</span><strong>7 of 20 (35%)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Most wins</span><strong>Menken (3), Barry &amp; Williams (2)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-dominant">
          <strong>Character:</strong> The most stylistically eclectic era — Vangelis' synthesizers (<em>Chariots of Fire</em>), Herbie Hancock's jazz (<em>Round Midnight</em>), a three-composer world-music collage (<em>The Last Emperor</em>). Yet the era is bookended by Hollywood norms: Alan Menken's Broadway-inflected Disney hat-trick dominated the early 1990s. The repeat-winner rate held steady, suggesting the field remained competitive despite stylistic diffusion.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card contemporary">
        <div class="era-stripe"></div>
        <div class="era-name">Contemporary</div>
        <div class="era-years">2001 – 2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 26 Ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Unique composers</span><strong>21</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Repeat-win ceremonies</span><strong>9 of 26 (35%)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span>Most wins</span><strong>Göransson (4)</strong></div>
        <div class="era-dominant">
          <strong>Character:</strong> The most globally diverse era on record. Winners have come from Argentina (Santaolalla), Canada (Shore), India (Rahman), Iceland (Guðnadóttir), Germany (Bertelmann), and Sweden (Göransson). Electronic and hybrid scores (Reznor/Ross, <em>Soul</em>; Price, <em>Gravity</em>) have won alongside traditional orchestral writing. Göransson's emergence as the era's dominant figure by its closing years is a data-clear trend.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      Across all four eras, the repeat-winner rate has been remarkably stable — ranging from 30–35%. This suggests a structural feature of the category: the pool of serious contenders in any given year is large enough to produce first-time winners most of the time, but dominant composers reliably find ways to return.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 5: MILESTONES ═══════════════ -->
<section id="milestones">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-num">05</span>
      <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="milestone-list">
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">1934</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">First Winner</div>
          <strong>Louis Silvers — One Night of Love</strong><br>
          The inaugural Best Original Score Oscar, presented at the 6th Academy Awards. Silvers never won again, joining the 85% majority of one-time winners.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">1950–51</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">First Back-to-Back</div>
          <strong>Franz Waxman — Sunset Boulevard &amp; A Place in the Sun</strong><br>
          The first and only back-to-back wins of the Golden Age. No composer managed this feat again until Alan Menken in 1991–92.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">1975–77</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">Blockbuster Era Opens</div>
          <strong>John Williams — Jaws (1975) &amp; Star Wars (1977)</strong><br>
          Two wins in three years for scores that redefined what film music could commercially achieve and accelerated Hollywood's embrace of neo-Romantic orchestral scoring.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">1989–92</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">Disney Dominance</div>
          <strong>Alan Menken — 3 wins in 4 years</strong><br>
          <em>The Little Mermaid</em> (1989), <em>Beauty and the Beast</em> (1991), <em>Aladdin</em> (1992). Includes the second-ever consecutive-year double (1991–92). Menken ultimately received 8 total nominations in this window, winning half.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">1994 → 2021</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">Longest Gap</div>
          <strong>Hans Zimmer — The Lion King to Dune: 27 years</strong><br>
          The longest verified gap between wins for any repeat winner in the dataset. Zimmer was nominated many times in the intervening years (Gladiator, Inception, Dunkirk, etc.) before finally returning with Dune.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">2015</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">Late-Career Milestone</div>
          <strong>Ennio Morricone — The Hateful Eight</strong><br>
          Morricone won his first competitive Oscar at the reported age of 87, making this one of the latest career wins in category history. (He had received an Honorary Oscar in 2007.)
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">2019</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">First Female Winner</div>
          <strong>Hildur Guðnadóttir — Joker</strong><br>
          The first woman to win the Best Original Score Oscar as a solo composer, in the category's 85th year of existence.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="milestone-item">
        <div class="milestone-year">2018–2026</div>
        <div class="milestone-content">
          <div class="milestone-tag">Active Record-Tier Run</div>
          <strong>Ludwig Göransson — 4 wins in 9 years</strong><br>
          <em>Black Panther</em> (2018), <em>Oppenheimer</em> (2023 &amp; 2024), <em>Sinners</em> (2026). The densest multi-win run of any composer in the category's history relative to elapsed time, with the 2023–24 back-to-back marking the fourth such occurrence ever. The dataset ends with Göransson still active.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS ═══════════════ -->
<section id="conclusions">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-num">06</span>
      <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="takeaway-list">
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num"></div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <div class="takeaway-heading">The category rewards longevity — but dominance is always era-specific.</div>
          Every era has produced one or two composers who temporarily towered above the competition: Tiomkin in the 1950s, Williams and Barry across the 1970s–90s, Menken in the early 1990s. None of these dominant runs persisted across more than about two decades. The structural pattern suggests that even the most decorated composers eventually yield ground to a new generation's sensibility — making the current Göransson run historically notable not just for its speed but for its potential longevity.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num"></div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <div class="takeaway-heading">The repeat-winner rate (~28%) has been structurally stable across all eras.</div>
          Whether in the emigré-dominated Golden Age, the stylistically eclectic Modern era, or the globally diverse Contemporary period, roughly one in four ceremonies goes to a repeat winner. This stability — despite wildly different competitive landscapes — implies something intrinsic to the category's voter dynamics: established composers retain a credibility premium, but the field is too large and competitive for any one figure to monopolize.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num"></div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <div class="takeaway-heading">Geographic and stylistic diversification is the clearest contemporary trend.</div>
          The pre-1980 record is almost entirely dominated by composers trained in the European Romantic tradition or the Broadway/Hollywood mainstream. By the 2000s, winners regularly came from Argentina, Canada, India, and the UK. By the 2020s, Iceland and Germany joined the list. This is not merely cosmetic diversity — the winning scores themselves reflect fundamentally different musical vocabularies. The Academy's voting base has broadened its frame of reference over five decades.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num"></div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <div class="takeaway-heading">Electronic and hybrid scoring has been fully legitimized — and then normalized.</div>
          Giorgio Moroder's win for <em>Midnight Express</em> (1978) was a shock. Vangelis for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981) confirmed it wasn't a fluke. By 2010, Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross winning for <em>The Social Network</em> — an entirely electronic score — no longer registered as controversial. The 2020s see hybrid approaches (Göransson's fusion work, Bertelmann's textural minimalism) treated on equal footing with traditional orchestral writing.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-num"></div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <div class="takeaway-heading">Ludwig Göransson's pace of wins is historically unprecedented — and unresolved.</div>
          Four wins in nine years (2018–2026), including a back-to-back, makes Göransson the fastest composer to reach the four-win record. Unlike John Barry (24-year span) or John Williams (18-year span), Göransson has achieved this with no apparent slowdown — the dataset ends at his most recent win. Whether this represents the opening of a sustained dominant run or its peak is, by definition, unknowable from the current data. It is the open question the record leaves behind.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

